# Pin line endings, because in this competition they are worth bytes.
#
# The packer deliberately KEEPS newlines — stripping them could let automatic
# semicolon insertion change what the code means, which is the one way a naive
# minifier breaks a game between the last playtest and the judge's browser. So
# every newline survives into the archive and gets deflated.
#
# That makes the checkout's line-ending policy part of the build. MEASURED
# 2026-08-22 on Windows, from identical source:
#
#     LF checkout    11,274 bytes zipped
#     CRLF checkout  11,449 bytes zipped   (+175, and not our doing)
#
# Without this file a `git clone` on a machine with core.autocrlf=true rebuilds
# a different archive from the one that was submitted — and for a game sitting
# closer to the cap than this one, that difference is the difference between a
# valid entry and a disqualified one.
* text=auto eol=lf
